name 'pl-aif-y2-u1-l3-f-strings'
title 'title'
description 'description here'

markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Formatted Strings (F-Strings)

Sometimes you want to include a variable's value inside text you're printing, not just the variable on its own.

## The Problem: This Doesn't Work

Here's what happens if you try the obvious way first.

<div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; margin: 12px 0 16px 0; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
  <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 20px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8;">
followers = 41<br>
print("The user is followed by followers people.")
  </div>
  <div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; padding: 10px 20px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #1F1976;">
The user is followed by followers people.
  </div>
</div>

<p>Python printed the word "followers" exactly as typed, it has no way of knowing you meant the variable, since it's just sitting inside a regular quoted string.</p>

## The Fix: F-Strings

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128214; F-string:</strong> a string that starts with the letter <code style="font-size:16px;">f</code> right before the opening quotation mark. Any variable name placed inside curly braces <code style="font-size:16px;">{ }</code> gets replaced with that variable's actual value.
  </div>
</div>

<div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; margin: 12px 0 16px 0; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
  <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 20px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8;">
followers = 41<br>
print(<span style="color:#ffffff; font-weight:700;">f</span>"The user is followed by <span style="color:#ffffff; font-weight:700;">{followers}</span> people.")
  </div>
  <div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; padding: 10px 20px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #1F1976;">
The user is followed by 41 people.
  </div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128161; Teacher tip:</strong> the syntax is small but easy to forget under pressure: an <code style="font-size:16px;">f</code> right before the quote, and curly braces around the variable name. Expect to remind students of exactly this when they hit it live.
  </div>
</div>

<p>Next, you'll trace and build this same pattern yourself in the Python Lab, exactly the kind of program students write in Lesson 4.</p>
MARKDOWN
